\chapter{Locations and useful diagnostics}
A source position is more useful when its meaning is precise. MuDSL spans are
half-open byte ranges: the beginning belongs to the range and the end does not.
Lines and columns are one-based. Byte columns avoid decoding ambiguities when
the source contains arbitrary UTF-8 or binary data.

\section{Source maps}
\api{SourceMap} records the start of each line. Its \api{line_column} operation
maps offsets to positions, and \api{line_text} retrieves a line for a diagnostic
excerpt. A default-constructed map represents one empty line. Offsets beyond
the source are clamped to EOF, including the largest representable offset.

\begin{code}
dsl::SourceMap source("alpha\nbeta");
auto [line, column] = source.line_column(6);
// line == 2, column == 1
\end{code}

The C++ map borrows its source view: retain the source storage while using the
map. D strings retain their backing storage through the garbage collector.

\section{Report context once}
Give a diagnostic a stable category and a concrete message. Prefer ``expected
closing parenthesis'' over ``parse failed.'' Include a source name when a host
runs multiple files. MuLL errors carry a message and line/column fields; the
embedding boundary adds the input filename. Native exceptions are translated
at the calling expression so a library failure still points to script code.

Test errors at EOF, after a newline, and with an empty source. These are common
places for accidental index underflow and inconsistent coordinate conventions.
